   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public officers law, in relation to the disclosure of law\nenforcement disciplinary records",
    "basePrintNo": "S5500",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5500-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 60,
       "fullName": "Patrick M. Gallivan",
       "imgName": "427_patrick_m._gallivan.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 427,
       "sessionMemberId": 2010,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "GALLIVAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Public Officers Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S5500\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public officers law, in relation to the disclosure\nof law enforcement disciplinary records\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo provide law enforcement officers with notice whenever their personal\ninformation has been released to the public pursuant to a FOIL request.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends section 87 of the Public Officers Law by\nadding a new subdivision 4-c to effectuate the purposes of the legis-\nlation. Section 2 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\nSection two is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nDuring the 2020 Legislative Session, legislation was enacted to repeal\nthe provisions of § 50-a of the Civil Rights Law and to amend § 86, §\n87, and § 89 of the Public Officers Law. These cumulative statutory\nchanges effectively removed privacy protections for our State's law\nenforcement officers by making their personnel records accessible to the\npublic via a simple FOIL request. While seeking to provide the public\nwith greater access to personnel files of police officers, corrections\nofficers, firefighters and paramedics, the Legislature failed to ensure\nthat these dedicated civil servants whose personal information is\nsubject to a FOIL request be provided any form of notice of such disclo-\nsure. This legislation simply recognizes that these impacted public\nemployees should have minimum notice when their personal information has\nbeen released to the public.\n\nThis proposed legislation would have no effect on the release of infor-\nmation that is now permissible following the changes to the Civil Rights\nLaw and the Public Officers Law in 2020. Rather, it would simply ensure\nthat if a public employee's personal information has been released to\nthe public, then such affected employee would have notice of such\nrelease.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
